# Voice and Multilingual Interaction

## Goal
Provide natural multilingual interaction while preserving a consistent iRiel voice identity and user control.

## Contracts
Separate speech recognition, language identification, translation, speech synthesis, and voice rendering. Each adapter declares supported locales, dialects, streaming behavior, latency, privacy mode, and on-device capability.

## Behavior
- Preserve the user's chosen language unless they request a switch or the conversation clearly changes.
- Distinguish language from region/dialect; do not assume nationality from accent.
- Adapt pronunciation, register, and prosody to locale while maintaining a stable voice identity.
- Allow user control over voice, speed, pitch where supported, and spoken/text output.
- Provide transcripts and editable text for recognized speech.
- Handle code-switching and names without silently normalizing away meaning.
- Ask before sending voice data to a remote service when local processing is unavailable or disallowed.

## Reliability
Show listening/processing/speaking states; support interruption, cancellation, replay, and graceful fallback to text. Never claim speech was transcribed or spoken without adapter confirmation.

## Tests
Portuguese BR/PT, English US/UK, Spanish and additional configured locales; mixed-language utterances; noisy input; names; interruption; offline mode; privacy denial; screen-reader coexistence.